      "rationale": "Comparing two collections of peso denominations is the right act, but the four-option items offer choices that are neither of the two collections named in the stem. In seed 42 the stem pits one collection against another, yet an option reads '1 ₱500 bill, 1 ₱100 bill, and 1 ₱50 bill', which the stem never mentioned and which therefore cannot be the answer to the question asked."
    },
    "comprehensive_coverage": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Greater-value and smaller-value prompts both occur, and coins and bills are mixed together. What never occurs is the equality case: 'They are equal' sits in almost every option list but is never once the key across these fifteen seeds. Nor is there any item comparing two single denominations against each other, which is the simplest form the competency's wording covers."
    },
    "cognitive_capacity": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Seed 46 asks a Grade 2 child to hold five denominations on one side against three on the other inside a single unpunctuated sentence where the pivotal 'or' is buried mid-clause. The arithmetic is within reach for this quarter; the parsing load of a five-item enumeration read aloud is what pushes past it."
    },
    "variant_comprehensiveness": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Four delivery formats appear (cloze, set_fill_in_blank, mcq, read_mcq) but they all carry one identical sentence shell beginning 'Which has a greater value:' or its smaller-value twin. No shopping or wallet scenario, no ordering of three amounts, no picture of actual currency is present."
    },
    "competency_alignment": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "Harder seeds genuinely stress the target skill: seed 605 makes the longer collection the larger one while seed 501 makes the longer collection the smaller, so a child cannot win by counting items instead of totalling value. That is the discriminating axis this competency needs."
    },
